---
description: Enforce the six-phase product lifecycle — no phase work before the previous phase is approved
alwaysApply: true
---

## What This Rule Does

`01-specify-rules.mdc` gates one **feature**. This rule gates the whole
**product**. It ensures nobody designs a system nobody specified, and nobody
builds a system nobody designed.

The two are nested, not competing:

```
PRODUCT LIFECYCLE  (this rule)      once per product
  1 REQUIREMENTS -> 2 ANALYSIS -> 3 DESIGN -> 4 DEVELOPMENT <-> 5 TESTING -> 6 PRODUCTION
                                                 |
                            FEATURE PIPELINE (01-specify-rules), once per feature
                            analyze -> clarify -> constitution -> plan -> specify
                                    -> implement -> checklist -> commit
```

Phases 4 and 5 are a loop, not two steps. A failing test returns work to
development; the lifecycle stays in `TESTING`.

---

## The State File Is The Truth

`lifecycle/state.json` records which phase the product is in and which gates a
human approved. It is machine-owned — `guard-write.mjs` blocks hand-edits.

```bash
node .cursor/tools/lifecycle.mjs status                    # where are we, and is it still true
node .cursor/tools/lifecycle.mjs check [PHASE]             # consent 1: artifacts exist
node .cursor/tools/lifecycle.mjs record-gate PHASE --verdict GO --by "lifecycle-gate"
                                                           # consent 2: the review verdict
node .cursor/tools/lifecycle.mjs approve PHASE --by "name"  # consent 3: the human
node .cursor/tools/lifecycle.mjs advance
node .cursor/tools/lifecycle.mjs rollback PHASE --reason "..."
```

## Three Consents, All Required

`approve` refuses unless all three are present, the phase is the current one,
and the previous phase is cleared:

| Consent | Recorded by | Proves |
|---|---|---|
| mechanical | `approve`, recomputed live | the artifacts exist and are not templates |
| judgement | `/lifecycle-gate` via `record-gate` | they are worth building on |
| human | `approve --by "<name>"` | someone is accountable |

## Status Is Derived, Not Stored

The state file holds evidence; the status is computed on every read.

| Status | Means |
|---|---|
| `NOT_STARTED` / `IN_PROGRESS` | no approval yet |
| `APPROVED` | all three consents, all still valid |
| `INHERITED` | brownfield; done informally before the lifecycle was adopted |
| `STALE` | **was** approved, and something it was approved against changed |
| `BLOCKED` | an earlier phase is not cleared |

`STALE` is the one that matters. Approval hashes every required artifact and
stamps the gate definition's version. Edit an approved design document, or
tighten a gate criterion, and the phase is `STALE` on the very next command —
and `guard-phase.mjs` starts blocking source writes again.

When a phase reads `STALE`, do not offer to re-approve it. Offer to re-review it:
the approval that lapsed was granted against text that no longer exists.

The `SessionStart` hook injects the current phase automatically. If a repo has
no `lifecycle/state.json` it never adopted the lifecycle, and this rule is
inert — that is a valid configuration, not a violation.

---

## Binding Behaviour

**Do not run a skill that belongs to a later phase than the current one.**
When asked to, say which phase the work belongs to, which phase the product is
in, and what would have to be approved first. Then offer to do the current
phase's work instead.

**Never mark a gate approved on the user's behalf.** `approve` requires
`--by "name"`. A gate a machine can clear is not a gate. Recording a review
verdict with `record-gate` is the agent's job; granting approval is not.

**Never propose an override.** There is no `--force`. A bypass is an `override`
record with an owner, a risk level and an expiry, and it exists for a human who
has decided to accept a specific risk. An agent suggesting one is the failure the
gate was built to prevent.

**Never soften a gate failure.** Same rule as `/production-readiness-review`:
a gate that has never blocked anything is a form, not a control.

**The write policy is enforced, not requested.** `guard-phase.mjs` returns exit
code 2 on any write the current phase does not permit. The policy is data, in
`.cursor/lifecycle/write-policy.json`:

| Artifact | Earliest phase | Needs cleared |
|---|---|---|
| `src/`, `backend/`, `frontend/` source | DEVELOPMENT | DESIGN |
| Database migrations | DEVELOPMENT | DESIGN |
| IaC — `*.tf`, `*.bicep`, `infra/` | DESIGN | ANALYSIS |
| CI pipelines — `.github/workflows/` | TESTING | DEVELOPMENT |
| Deployment manifests — `k8s/`, `helm/` | PRODUCTION | TESTING |

`earliest: X` means the phase **before** X must be `APPROVED` or `INHERITED` —
`STALE` included, so an edited design document re-blocks source on the next call.

Never blocked: `tests/`, `specs/`, `docs/`, `memory-bank/`, `.cursor/`,
`.claude/`, `*.md`. Writing a test or a spec early is good practice, not a
violation. If it blocks you,
stop and tell the user why. Do not route around it with `Bash`.

Tests, specs, docs and `memory-bank/` are never blocked — writing a test or a
spec before the design gate is good practice, not a violation.

