## Promotion, Not Duplication

Phases 1-3 must not create a second home for truth that `memory-bank/` already
holds. Each gate **promotes** its decisions into the Tier 2 files the guard
rules already read:

| Phase | Promotes into |
|---|---|
| 1 Requirements | `projectbrief.md`, `productContext.md`, `businessRules.md` |
| 2 Analysis | `businessRules.md`, `glossary.md` (EN/AR terms) |
| 3 Design | `architecture.md`, `technologyStack.md`, `databaseConventions.md`, `securityStandards.md` |

This is what makes a design decision binding on the code rather than merely
adjacent to it — once it is in `memory-bank/architecture.md`, rule 02 enforces it.

Tier 2 files are human-authored: `guard-write.mjs` blocks agent writes to them.
So promotion is an explicit step — show the diff, get the user's word, then apply
with `CLAUDE_ALLOW_TIER2_EDIT=1`. Never promote silently.

---

## Brownfield Repos

A codebase that already exists did not skip phases 1-3; it did them informally,
years ago, in people's heads. `lifecycle.mjs init --existing` records that
honestly as `INHERITED` rather than either pretending they were approved or
blocking all work on a live system.

`INHERITED` clears the design gate. To upgrade a phase from `INHERITED` to
`APPROVED`, produce its artifacts — `/feature-inventory` and `/context-sync`
reconstruct most of phases 1-2 from the code — then run `check` and `approve`.
